# =============================================================================
# recipient-exclusions.yml.example — the framework-shippable TWIN
# =============================================================================
#
# Copy to instance/config/recipient-exclusions.yml and have the Captain ratify
# it. This example ships an EMPTY denylist: exclusions are CAPTAIN rulings,
# never framework defaults. Ruling provenance belongs in the Captain ledger
# (shared/interfaces/captain-decisions.md) — cite it from `why:`.
#
# WHY THIS FILE EXISTS
# --------------------
# `org_domains:` in instance/config/platform.yml is ALREADY the allowlist of
# what counts as internal, and the framework ships none of its own (absent
# config ⇒ every recipient external). What it cannot express is an EXCEPTION.
# A listed domain admits every address at it, so a distribution list or alias
# that fans out to non-employees is internal by construction, and — before
# 2026-07-27 — every subdomain of it was internal too, unboundedly and
# forever, including subdomains that do not exist yet or are operated by
# someone else. This file is the one place to say "not that one".
#
# The consumer is framework/authority/classifier.py `_is_internal_recipient`
# (via framework/env.py `recipient_policy`). Everything here can only make a
# recipient EXTERNAL — which is the always-gated ceiling, i.e. more Captain
# approvals, never fewer. There is deliberately NO way to make a recipient
# internal from this file; that is what org_domains is for.
#
# CORRUPTION-HONESTY CONTRACT (the consumer's fail-shape, do not weaken):
#   * File ABSENT           ⇒ empty denylist (no exclusions; org_domains
#                             alone decides, as it did before this file).
#   * File PRESENT but
#     UNPARSEABLE/DAMAGED   ⇒ EVERY recipient classifies external until
#                             repaired — an unreadable Captain exclusion list
#                             is NEVER silently ignored.
#     "Damaged" means anything that could silently SHRINK the exclusion set: a
#     non-mapping document, a dropped or non-list `denylist:` key, a row that
#     is not a mapping, a row with neither or both of address/domain, an empty
#     value, a value contradicting its key (an `address` with no `@`), or an
#     unrecognized `subdomain_matching` value. An explicitly empty
#     `denylist: []` is the ruled posture and stays valid.
#
#     Also refused, each found by attacking the first version of this parser:
#       * a DUPLICATE `denylist:` key — YAML takes last-wins silently, so
#         appending a second one empties your list while every original row
#         still reads intact above it.
#       * YAML anchors/aliases — an exclusion list whose real content is
#         assembled from anchors elsewhere in the document cannot be audited
#         by eye, which is the only thing this file is for.
#       * a dangling symlink at this path — that is a file PRESENT and
#         unreadable, i.e. damaged, not absent.
#       * a row that parses but can NEVER MATCH, which would read live and
#         exclude nothing: any value carrying whitespace/comma/semicolon or
#         angle brackets (including `Nate <list@example.com>` pasted out of a
#         mail client), and a domain written with a leading or trailing dot.
#         Write the bare address or the bare domain, one per row.
#       * a file over 1 MiB — it is parsed at startup and would stall every
#         classification.
#     `subdomain_matching` IS case-folded, so a caps typo cannot take the whole
#     cabinet to deny-all.
#
# WHAT THIS CANNOT DO
# -------------------
# It cannot see a delivery graph. The classifier is handed a string of
# addresses, never the mailboxes behind them. An internal-looking address that
# relays, forwards or fans out to outsiders stays internal until a human
# denylists it here by hand. Listing the ones you know of is the whole
# mechanism — there is no detection.
# =============================================================================

version: 1
status: unratified                # flips to `ruled` when the Captain ratifies
ruled_at: ""

# --- how a recipient domain matches an org_domains entry ---------------------
#   strict  (DEFAULT, and what ships) — a recipient domain is internal only if
#           it EXACTLY equals a listed org domain. A subdomain you want treated
#           as internal gets its own line in org_domains. Safe by default: a
#           domain you list never silently annexes subdomains you do not
#           control, and never annexes ones created later.
#   inherit — the pre-2026-07-27 framework behaviour: exact match OR any
#           subdomain, unboundedly. Reachable here so a deployment that relies
#           on it needs a config line, not a code change.
#
# MIGRATION, if you are moving an existing deployment onto this file: under
# `strict`, recipients at subdomains of your listed domains flip from internal
# to external, so they start requiring Captain approval. Nothing escapes — the
# failure mode is approval noise, and it is loud and immediate. Fix it either
# by adding the subdomain to org_domains (preferred: it is then a named,
# auditable claim) or by setting `inherit` below (restores the old blanket).
subdomain_matching: strict

# =============================================================================
# DENYLIST — Captain exclusions. EMPTY until the Captain rules.
#
# Each row carries EXACTLY ONE of:
#   address: "list@example.com"   an exact address (must contain @)
#   domain:  "partner.example.com" that domain AND every subdomain of it
# plus a `why:` naming the ruling. `why` is a documented obligation, not a
# machine gate — a row missing it still excludes, because a forgotten `why`
# must never turn an urgent exclusion into a deny-all outage.
#
# Worked examples (delete these; they are illustration, not defaults):
#   denylist:
#     - address: "all-staff@example.com"
#       why: "distribution list — fans out to contractors and board observers;
#             a send here leaves the org. Captain ruling YYYY-MM-DD."
#     - domain: "newsroom.example.com"
#       why: "subdomain operated by an external agency since the YYYY sale.
#             Captain ruling YYYY-MM-DD."
# =============================================================================
denylist: []
